### [13:12:40 UTC] Step 4/5/6c — measured coherence checks + spectral analysis on the verified output
Input: output_test.flac (verified remote-run output, 176400 Hz / 2ch / 15.137 s / PCM_24 / 3549564 bytes), reference input_test.flac (308700 Hz / 2ch / 15.137 s / PCM_24), source input_test.mp3 (44100 Hz / 2ch / 192 kbps / 15.177 s). Scripts /tmp/aqc/analyze.py and /tmp/aqc/hf_detail.py (scipy/numpy/soundfile only, no librosa).
Action: container inspection; per-channel raw sample checks; above-original-Nyquist band report (Hann-windowed full-length rFFT per channel); mono downmix + resample_poly(7/4) of the output to the reference's 308700 Hz, trim to min length (4,672,878 samples = 15.137 s); clipping fraction, invalid samples, near-zero-run dropout matching (50 ms / |x|<1e-4), sample-diff 99.9th-percentile discontinuity comparison; scipy.signal.stft(nperseg=2048, noverlap=1024) magnitudes, per-max normalization, spectral convergence + Pearson correlation; per-octave-band mean-magnitude deltas; 50 ms frame-RMS dynamic-range comparison; then a follow-up test of whether the added 16-22 kHz energy is content-following or a static/uniform tilt.
Reasoning: every score must trace to a measured number; the added-detail vs. noise-floor-rise distinction needed the extra time-resolved test before I could call the top-octave rise "detail" or "tilt".
Output: NO local GPU was used and no local upscale() was run -- all measurements are against the remote job's committed output.
  Container: sample rate 176400 = 44100 * 4 (compute_upscale_factor: round(1400000/192000)=7 clamped by 192000//44100=4); channels 2 preserved; duration 15.13728 s == reference exactly, 0.0399 s under the mp3's mutagen-reported 15.1771 s (encoder padding, expected); PCM_24; real bitrate 1875.9 kbps.
  Raw output: peak 0.9999999, RMS 0.1941, 0 NaN, 0 Inf, clipping fraction 1.286e-04, DC offset 1.6e-05, per-channel RMS 0.1945 / 0.1936 (balanced).
  Above original Nyquist (22050 Hz): peak -148.99 dB (L) / -138.43 dB (R) relative to in-band peak; band RMS -187.1 / -186.8 dB; the peak bin sits at 22050.07 Hz, i.e. the first bin past the cutoff (spectral leakage), not an image/harmonic. FFT noise floor -> hard constraint SATISFIED.
  NOTE on the reference: input_test.flac ITSELF carries above-22050 Hz content at -57.4 dB peak @ 37298 Hz (legacy artifact of an older 7x ZOH-era pipeline output; it is a 308700 Hz file). This is a property of the reference file, not of this run's output; masked out of the spectrogram image per the rules.
  Aligned checks: clipping output 3.70e-05 vs reference 1.69e-04 (output cleaner); 0 NaN/Inf; dropouts 0 runs in output and 0 in reference, 0 unmatched; discontinuities p99.9 output 0.0161 vs reference 0.0582 (ratio 0.277), max 0.0428 vs 0.2522 (ratio 0.170) -- output is SMOOTHER than the reference, no clicks/pops introduced.
  Spectral deviation: spectral_convergence=0.9839, correlation=0.99988, combined=0.99190 -> score 9.9. Time-domain waveform Pearson 0.99975.
  Added detail below 22050 Hz: mean delta +1.24 dB overall, +2.86 dB in the reference's quietest quartile of bands (16279-22007 Hz) vs +0.098 dB in its loudest quartile; octave deltas -0.01/-0.01/-0.00/+0.02/+0.09/+0.28/+0.73/+1.37/+2.30/+3.37 dB from 0-200 Hz up to 20-22.05 kHz. No broadband rise (in-band median magnitude -78.6/-78.2 dB rel peak vs reference -79.6/-79.1 dB, ~1 dB).
  Follow-up (hf_detail.py): the 16-22 kHz delta is near-uniform in time (p5 +1.95 dB, p95 +2.95 dB across 4565 STFT frames), the band's own dynamic range is preserved (45.5 dB output vs 45.8 dB reference) and corr(output HF envelope, reference HF envelope) = 0.85 -- so this is a small, roughly constant HF level/tilt riding on the reference's own HF envelope, NOT newly reconstructed content-derived detail. At -95.6 dB mean it is inaudible either way.
  Dynamic range: p95-p5 frame RMS 27.011 dB (output) vs 27.007 dB (reference); min frame -69.380 vs -69.381 dB -> no static floor, no dynamic-range collapse (contrast cycle 3's 57.1 -> 25.4 dB regression).
  Scoring: coherence = 9 (clean on every check incl. above-Nyquist, but the only "added" energy is a uniform inaudible HF tilt rather than clearly measurable added detail -> the 8-9 "safe, transparent upscale" row, at its top). spectral_deviation = 9.9.

